   "summary": "Unlock these with Subscription Delhi University on Friday approved a proposal to provide ₹17 crore in financial assistance for the treatment of the nine-month-old daughter of a faculty member at the School of Open Learning (SOL). The child has spinal muscular atrophy (SMA) Type II, a rare genetic disorder. According to the Union Health Ministry, SMA is a rare genetic disorder that affects nerve cells in the spinal cord, causing muscle weakness in the limbs and trunk.",
   "summary_zh": "解锁这些内容需订阅。德里大学于周五批准了一项提案，将提供1,700万卢比经济援助，用于治疗开放学习学院（SOL）一名教职员工的九个月大的女儿。该名儿童患有脊髓性肌萎缩症（SMA）II型，这是一种罕见的遗传疾病。据联邦卫生部称，脊髓性肌萎缩症是一种影响脊髓神经细胞的罕见遗传疾病，会导致四肢和躯干肌肉无力。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Delhi University on Friday approved a proposal to provide ₹17 crore in financial assistance for the treatment of the nine-month-old daughter of a faculty member at the School of Open Learning (SOL). The child has spinal muscular atrophy (SMA) Type II, a rare genetic disorder. SOL officials said Vice-Chancellor Yogesh Singh had constituted a special committee, which recommended the financial assistance. Before the decision, the SOL Academic Association had launched a crowdfunding initiative that raised ₹3.11 crore through contributions from the Tata Trust, the Chief Minister’s Relief Fund, online platforms such as Impact Guru, Milaap, Ketto and GoFundMe, the Teacher Relief Fund and SOL’s non-academic staff association. Payal Mago, Director of SOL, said the coordinated efforts of university faculty and prompt action had helped secure financial assistance for the child’s treatment within the required timeframe. She said any delay could have put the child’s life at risk. According to the Union Health Ministry, SMA is a rare genetic disorder that affects nerve cells in the spinal cord, causing muscle weakness in the limbs and trunk. New treatment options have improved the outlook for patients with the condition. Symptoms of SMA Type I typically appear before six months of age, causing muscle weakness and limited limb movement. Without treatment, affected children may die of respiratory failure before the age of two. Type II manifests between six months and two years of age. Affected children can sit unaided but generally require wheelchairs and often develop spinal deformities after adolescence. Those who develop the condition later in childhood or adulthood may remain mobile for longer. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
